      "content": "Yes, it is indeed a delicate balance, and signs often appear as a vinegar smell or an overly slimy texture rather than the usual pleasant sourdough scent. If the yeast overwhelms the bacteria due to excess warm sugar, the bread may rise too quickly, turn dark, and lack acidic flavor, while an imbalance toward bacteria can cause tight crumb or unwanted predominance of acetic acid. To salvage this, you need to discard a portion of the problematic starter and refresh it with fresh flour and water to reset the microbial population.",
